{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1300,
    "dDurationMs": 4179,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From this photo it’s easy to see that not\nall parts of a flame are made the same – they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5479,
    "dDurationMs": 3801,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can be brighter around the edges and darker\nin the middle. Which is weird, because a flame"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9280,
    "dDurationMs": 2910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is normally *brightest* in the middle, since\nthat’s where you’re looking at the most"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12190,
    "dDurationMs": 990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hot burning gas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13180,
    "dDurationMs": 420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mean,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13600,
    "dDurationMs": 3370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Look at the sun – actually, don’t do that\nwithout a solar filter – but, it’s a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16970,
    "dDurationMs": 4430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "big ball of hot glowing gas, and it’s definitely\nbrightest in the middle. In fact, not only"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21400,
    "dDurationMs": 4359,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "does it get darker towards the edges, it gets\nredder. These dark, red edges of the sun are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25759,
    "dDurationMs": 3850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "called “limbs,” since “limbus” is\nlatin for “edge”. And the limbs of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29609,
    "dDurationMs": 3220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sun get darker and redder near the edge for\nthree simple reasons:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32829,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First, the sun is cooler closer to its “surface”\nand hotter deeper in."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36769,
    "dDurationMs": 4491,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Second, hotter stuff glows brighter and yellower,\nwhich you’ve probably seen with a hot poker"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41260,
    "dDurationMs": 1180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or candle flame."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42440,
    "dDurationMs": 3220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And third, if you look across the edge of\na sphere, you have to look through more of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45660,
    "dDurationMs": 3449,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the sphere for your line of sight to reach\na given depth than when you look directly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49109,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into the middle. If the sphere is only partially\ntransparent (like the sun), when looking at"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53329,
    "dDurationMs": 3181,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the edge, you won’t see as deep into the\ndepths before your vision is blocked."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56510,
    "dDurationMs": 4180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, when you look directly – through a solar\nfilter – into the middle of the sun, you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60690,
    "dDurationMs": 3869,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "see deeper in to hot gas which glows bright\nand yellow. And when you look at the edge"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64559,
    "dDurationMs": 4411,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the sun, you see shallower, cooler, gas\nwhich glows darker and redder."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68970,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The same effect in reverse explains why some\nflames have bright edges and darker centers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73160,
    "dDurationMs": 4300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "– these flames are clearly hotter and brighter\nnear their surfaces, likely because that’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77460,
    "dDurationMs": 3980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where the best mixture of oxygen and fuel\nis, so when we look at their edges, our line"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81440,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of sight passes through more hot light-emitting\ngases than when we look straight-on. Take"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85720,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a candelabra and turn it side-on, and you’ll\nsee exactly what’s going on."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92060,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The optical effect I describe in the video is called limb darkening"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95000,
    "dDurationMs": 1940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or limb brightening."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96940,
    "dDurationMs": 2060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It also explains the theory the appearance observe nebulae"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99000,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which are actually hollow expanding shell of glowing gases"
    } ]
  } ]
}
